{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-78620",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The notation $(f/g)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x).$ \n\nTo evaluate $(f/g)(-1),$ we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1),$ and then divide the results. \n\nFirst, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =(-1)^{2}+2(-1)=-1 \\\\ g(-1) & =(-1)^{2}+(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHowever, since the denominator of a fraction can't be zero, we can't divide $f(-1)$ by $g(-1).$\n\nTherefore, the value  $(f/g)(-1)$  is undefined.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
